<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<root
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="exam.xsd"
>
  <exam id="1" shortname="DCNA" code="DC-01">
    <title>DN42 Certified Network Associate</title>
    <description>Passing this exam earns you the DCNA certification, and can also can be used towards your recertification goals.
    Mastering DN42 techniques (DC-01 DCNA) v1.1 is a 120-minute exam that tests a candidate's knowledge and skills related to network fundamentals, routing and switching, IP connectivity, BGP configuration, connectivity troubleshooting, and automation and programmability.</description>
    <questionset>
      <!-- A question set can contain multiple question collection candidate, to improve uncertainty by picking random question collection out of a multi-collection question set. -->
      <questioncollection>
        <question id="1" type="single-choice">
          <description>An engineer is configuring router R2 in autonomous system 65002 to control the propagation of the prefix 192.0.2.0/24, which R2 learns from router R1 via an eBGP session. The engineer applies an inbound route map that sets the well-known community NO_ADVERTISE on this prefix. R2 has three fully established iBGP peers inside AS 65002 and one additional eBGP peer in AS 65003. Assuming no other filtering is configured, to which peers will R2 advertise the 192.0.2.0/24 prefix?</description>
          <options>
            <option
              id="1"
            >All iBGP and eBGP peers, because NO_ADVERTISE only prevents the prefix from being readvertised toward the originating AS</option>
            <option id="2">All three iBGP peers only</option>
            <option id="3">The eBGP peer in AS 65003 only</option>
            <option
              id="4"
            >All iBGP and eBGP peers, but with the community attribute stripped from the updates</option>
            <option
              id="5"
            >No BGP peers at all; the prefix remains in R2's local BGP table but is not advertised to any peer</option>
          </options>
          <correctanswer>
            <options>
              <!-- In some case, more that one option is correct, even it's a single-choice question. -->
              <option
                id="5"
              >No BGP peers at all; the prefix remains in R2's local BGP table but is not advertised to any peer</option>
            </options>
          </correctanswer>
        </question>
        <question id="2" type="multiple-choice">
          <description>Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must configure an L3Out peering with the backbone network. The L3Out must forward unicast and multicast traffic over the link.
            Which two methods should be used to configure L3Out to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)</description>
          <exhibits>
            <exhibit>
              <image src="assets/9b44f37d-60b7-4b05-82db-2effb6aa58d6.jpg" />
            </exhibit>
          </exhibits>
          <options>
            <option id="1">Layer 3 routed port</option>
            <option id="2">VPC with SVI</option>
            <option id="3">port channel with SVI</option>
            <option id="4">Layer 3 routed subinterface</option>
            <option id="5">Layer 3 floating SVI</option>
          </options>
          <correctanswer>
            <!-- combinations are orderless, in other words: they are just unordered set. -->
            <!-- so that means, both (1, 4) and (4, 1) are correct answers. -->
            <combination>
              <option id="1" />
              <option id="4" />
            </combination>
          </correctanswer>
        </question>
        <question id="3" type="drag-and-drop">
          <description>An engineer deploys a Cisco ACI fabric. The engineer created a VPC policy group named Allot_MET_RIO7_RIO8 for the enclosure and these objects have been configured:
            &#8226; Physical Domain Bare_Metal_L3In_Dom
            &#8226; VLAN pool NFVI_VLPool
            &#8226; AAEP NFVI_AAEP
            Drag and drop objects from the left to the objects under which they must be added to configure an EPG named 12_MSSMET2_LDE_int_EPG. Not all objects are used.</description>
          <candidates>
            <candidate id="1">NFVI_VLPool</candidate>
            <candidate id="2">Bare_Metal_L3ln_Dom</candidate>
            <candidate id="3">NFVI_AAEP</candidate>
            <candidate id="4">Allot_MET_RIO7_RIO8</candidate>
          </candidates>
          <drops>
            <drop id="1">AAEP</drop>
            <drop id="2">physical domain</drop>
            <drop id="3">VPC policy group</drop>
          </drops>
          <correctanswer>
            <connectionsolutions>
              <connectionsolution requiredUniqueConnections="3">
                <!-- drag 2 and drop it to 1 -->
                <connect dst="1" src="2" />

                <!-- drag 1 and drop it to 2 -->
                <connect dst="2" src="1" />

                <!-- drag 3 and drop it to 3 -->
                <connect dst="3" src="3" />
              </connectionsolution>

              <!-- in some case, a drag-and-drop question can also have multiple correct answers, then we just define more connectionsolution objects -->
            </connectionsolutions>
          </correctanswer>
        </question>
        <question id="4" type="single-choice">
          <description>Refer to the exhibit. An engineer is investigating a reachability issue in an experimental BGP overlay network. ICMPv6 echo requests from the local router to fdea:6e46:2f1c::1 time out, and traceroute dies several hops into the path. The engineer verifies that the iBGP session to i-bzip9 is established, that no ACLs are blocking ICMPv6, and the rp_filter kernel parameter is set to 0 for all interfaces. Which statement best explains why the pings fail?</description>
          <exhibits>
            <exhibit>
              <image src="assets/83b8f88a-0cc4-439f-b838-dca4b015a376.png" />
            </exhibit>
          </exhibits>
          <options>
            <option
              id="1"
            >A link-local address cannot be used as the BGP next hop for a unicast prefix.</option>
            <option
              id="2"
            >The MED value of 100 prevents the route from being installed into the routing table.</option>
            <option
              id="3"
            >The entry is a ghost route, and traffic is blackholed along the phantom path.</option>
            <option
              id="4"
            >The community (64511,33) instructs downstream autonomous systems to filter the prefix.</option>
            <option
              id="5"
            >The asterisk (*) indicates the route is valid but not selected as best, so it is not used for forwarding.</option>
          </options>
          <correctanswer>
            <options>
              <option
                id="3"
              >The entry is a ghost route, and traffic is blackholed along the phantom path.</option>
            </options>
          </correctanswer>
        </question>
        <question id="5" type="drag-and-drop">
          <description>Drag and drop the Cisco ACI filter entry options from the left onto the correct categories on the right indicating what are required or optional parameters.
            Select and Place:</description>
          <candidates>
            <candidate id="1">Name</candidate>
            <candidate id="2">ARP Flag</candidate>
            <candidate id="3">Ether Type</candidate>
            <candidate id="4">IP Protocol</candidate>
            <candidate id="5">Source Port From</candidate>
          </candidates>
          <multiareadrop>
            <!-- in a multi-area drop, drop entries share the same id namespace, so even two drops are in different drop areas, their Ids still mustn't be duplicated -->
            <droparea id="drop-area-1">
              <droparealabel>Optional Parameters</droparealabel>
              <drop id="1" />
              <drop id="2" />
              <drop id="3" />
            </droparea>
            <droparea id="drop-area-2">
              <droparealabel>Required Parameters</droparealabel>
              <drop id="4" />
              <drop id="5" />
            </droparea>
          </multiareadrop>
          <correctanswer>
            <connectionsolutions>
              <!-- 5 unique connections are required -->
              <connectionsolution requiredUniqueConnections="5">

                <!--
                    A connectcombination object simply represents a list of Cartesian products yield from cross-multiplying the connectsources and connectdestinations
                    In this example, connect sources are: 1, 3; connect destinations are 4, 5
                    so the list of cartesian products would be:
                    { (1, 4), (1, 5), (3, 4), (3, 5) }
                    any connection falls within this set is considered correct
                    -->
                <connectcombination>
                  <connectsource id="1" />
                  <connectsource id="3" />
                  <connectdestination id="4" />
                  <connectdestination id="5" />
                </connectcombination>
                <connectcombination>
                  <connectsource id="2" />
                  <connectsource id="4" />
                  <connectsource id="5" />
                  <connectdestination id="1" />
                  <connectdestination id="2" />
                  <connectdestination id="3" />
                </connectcombination>
              </connectionsolution>

              <!-- in some case, a drag-and-drop question can also have multiple correct answers, then we just define more connectionsolution objects -->
            </connectionsolutions>
          </correctanswer>
        </question>
      </questioncollection>
    </questionset>
  </exam>
  <!-- 
    we write some example answers below, they are not the standard answer, 
    which is included in eact question object under the exam's hierachy
     -->
  <examanswer>
    <answer questionId="1" questionType="single-choice">
      <options>
        <option id="1" />
      </options>
    </answer>
    <answer questionId="2" questionType="multiple-choice">
      <options>
        <option id="1" />
        <option id="2" />
      </options>
    </answer>
    <answer questionId="3" questionType="drag-and-drop">
      <connections>
        <connect dst="1" src="2" />
        <connect dst="2" src="1" />
        <connect dst="3" src="3" />
      </connections>
    </answer>
  </examanswer>
